Since 2010, Apple has made privacy a big part of its brand. Early on, iPhones gave users only limited control over their data. Over time, Apple added stronger protections. For example, in 2014 Apple started
encrypting iPhones by default, so even Apple couldn’t unlock them without the user’s passcode. Later, features like “App Tracking Transparency” (2021) gave people the power to stop apps from tracking them across other
apps and websites. Apple has also built privacy labels on the App Store, letting users see what kind of data each app collects before downloading. Apple made these changes partly because people became more 
concerned about how companies use their data, and also because of pressure from governments and regulators. Data leaks, social media scandals, and growing awareness of targeted advertising made privacy a bigger issue,
and Apple saw a chance to stand out from competitors by focusing on protecting users. Are these protections enough? They’re strong, but not perfect. Apple still collects some data for services like iCloud and
advertising, and researchers have found limits to how private iPhones really are. Still, Apple does more than most companies to put privacy in the spotlight. Compared to Google’s Android devices, iPhones generally 
offer better default privacy. Google’s business model relies more on advertising, which means more data collection. While Android phones can be made private with settings or third-party tools, Apple makes privacy 
easier by default.
